a prayer that's way more than just a  prayer a mysterious dragon too big for  this world house of the dragon is  heating up here's what you may have  missed in episode 2.  episode 2 begins with some gnarly images  of sea life cleaning the flesh off the  bodies of sailors who appear to have  washed ashore in the next scene a  furious cordless valarian bursts into a  small council meeting and says something  must be done about the stepstones the  free cities and the crab feeder four  ships have now been lost the last one  was flying my banner otto hightower's  solution is to pay off the families of  the victims and refund corliss for his  losses but the seastank doesn't think  this course of action goes far enough in  the premiere corliss speaks of the  triarchy and someone named cragus drayar  in episode 2 when corliss asks damon if  he's heard about the trouble in the  stepstones in an attempt to recruit him  into the war he wants damon replies that  he has heard something about some  mirrish prince crakas the crab feeder  and this prince are one in the same and  the triarchy is the free cities of lis  mir and tirosh the crab feeder came by  his nickname honestly feeding sailors to  the craps might seem like a metaphor for  sea warfare but cragus literally feeds  his victims to his namesakes by fixing  their bodies to stakes at low tide and  letting the crabs do their thing the  source material george rr martin's fire  and blood leaves more to the imagination  and simply explains that the name was  related to the inventive punishments he  inflicted upon his enemies  history buff king viserys is building or  at least commissioning an elaborate  model of valyria the home of the  targaryens and the other now extinct  dragon riding families a city of a  thousand years  and all that men had learned  the doom consumed it all alike and  neither of them turned during one of  their visits in episode 2 he explains  his project to the young lady who's  obviously been sent to seduce him  alison's hightower the daughter of the  hand of the king viserys waxes poetic  about how valyria was built into a  volcano like dragonstone so its people  could be close to the source of their  magic and power he tells of blood mages  and a civilization that was one thousand  dragons strong do you believe the  westeros can be another valyria of grace  the king answers that it depends on  whether she means valyria at its peak or  at its fall referring to the doom of  valyria though martin has not yet  written a definitive account of exactly  what happened valeria suffered some  apocalyptic event about a dozen years  after the targaryens fled thanks to the  prophetic vision of danis targaryen some  characters speak of the doom as if it  was an unavoidable natural disaster but  the strong implication is that the  valyrians brought collapse upon  themselves with their lust for magic and  power viserys considers his own kingdom  with 10 dragons already falling into  disrepair after 100 years the glory of  old valeria will never be seen again  maybe not the glory but perhaps the  hubris in ruin the ceres thinks ruling  over peace means throwing feasts in  turnies not making difficult but  necessary decisions meanwhile the seven  hells are about to break loose it's  incredibly symbolic when he drops and  breaks a dragon figurine and mistakes  alison's ambition for kindness when she  has it fixed for thousands of years the  valyrians were  the best in the world that almost  everything  then  and then they weren't  episode two makes it clear that  allison's hightower is a willing  participant and not just a pawn in the  schemes that revolve around her though  the nervous fingernail picking suggests  she certainly has mixed feelings the  main plot line revolves around alison  and her father otto's efforts to  convince rhaenyra that her father the  king should remarry and to make sure he  chooses the teenage alicent over the 12  year old laina valerian who's the better  candidate on paper to soften up her  bestie alison has to mediate the  reconciliation between rhaenyra and her  father who've barely spoken since her  mother and brother's traumatic deaths  step one alison takes her to the sept to  pray to the seven in an attempt to get  some closure about it the scene is much  more loaded with subtext and  foreshadowing than it might seem upon  first watch the hightowers are the lords  of old town where they were once the  richest and most powerful family in  westeros the faith of the seven the  predominant religion in westeros also  calls old town home and the two entities  are closely aligned the hightowers  support the faith and vice versa which  increases both the noble family and the  new religion's political influence in  contrast the targaryens have a tenuous  relationship with the seven publicly  they've converted but really they keep  to their ancient customs and do their  best to limit the seven's role in the  culture of westeros by getting rhaenyra  to bend the knee in prayer alison is  flexing her own strength and using the  religion to which she's theoretically  much more devoted than rhaenyra for  cynical purposes what do i say  whatever you wish  it's only for you in the gods to know  showrunners have promised that we'll see  at least 17 dragons throughout the run  of house of the dragon including at  least nine in the first season so far  we've gotten glimpses of rhaenyra's  elegant mount cyrax and damon's fierce  red steed karaxis also known as the  bloodworm we've also heard about  valerian the black dread whose skull now  rests in the cellars of the red keep and  dreamfire whose egg was chosen for  rhaenyra's brother then stolen by damon  on his walk with lena valerian the tween  princess asks the king what it was like  to ride valerian some backstory viserys  was the last of the targaryen kings to  ride the black dread and he never bonded  with or rode another dragon afterwards  that means it's been quite some time  since the king was on dragonback which  is itself a symbol of his supposed  weakness then laina inquires about vegar  who she says was too big for the dragon  pit viserys retorts that she was perhaps  too big for this world vegar was a  female dragon almost as large as  balerion who was ridden by one of king  aegon's sister wives visenya long story  short vegar the biggest dragon in the  world is still out there and no one  knows for sure where she's nesting this  conversation isn't just scene filler  veigar is basically chekhov's dragon  sure to show up at some critical moment  in the future  after king viserys slices his finger on  the iron throne in episode 1 we see in  episode two that what started out as a  small cut has rotted into a serious  health situation though it occurs at a  different point in the narrative in fire  and blood the ceres cuts his digit so  badly that it needs to be amputated the  incident scars him physically and  mentally so much so that in the book  king viserys never sits on the iron  throne again in house of the dragon the  maesters recommend maggots to eat away  at the gangrene both versions come to  the same metaphorical conclusion the  wound will not heal viserys himself  utters this exact phrase in another  context later in the episode  your mother's absence  is a wound that will never heal he adds  that in queen emma's absence the red  keep will never recover and he's right  had she lived viserys would have  remained married to her and probably  wouldn't have produced another heir  rhaenyra's claim would be absolute save  for damon's insolence at being passed  over with emma out of the picture king  viserys has to choose between two  factions one led by the hightowers and  one led by the valerians since neither  side will ever accept the promotion of  the other over their own interests civil  war is inevitable
 

